# Secret hidden one base64 layer deep (unwrap-then-scan).
#
# Why this exists: a secret wrapped in a base64 blob is invisible to a surface
# scan — gitleaks/trufflehog match the DECODED shape of known providers, so a
# base64 envelope hides the inner {"s":<secret>} from them. The motivating case
# was a Cloudflare tunnel connector token committed as base64 JSON
# (eyJhIjoi... = {"a":"...). This rule decodes one base64 layer and re-scans the
# decoded text for secret shapes.
#
# How: engine=base64-unwrap finds base64/base64url runs (length >= min_count,
# default 24), decodes ONE layer, drops non-UTF-8/binary output, then runs the
# patterns below over the DECODED text. The finding points at the ENCODED line;
# the decoded secret is never echoed.
#
# The patterns are the INNER (decoded) shapes to look for, not the encoded blob.
#
# ERROR / block: a credential-shaped value inside an encoded blob is high signal.
#
# @see ADR-049 (base64 unwrap engine), CVE/real finding: cloudflared token in IaC.
id: SECRETS-090
title: Secret hidden inside a base64 blob (decode-and-scan)
category: secret
severity: error
engine: base64-unwrap
action: block
min_count: 24
confidence: 0.8
patterns:
  # Cloudflare tunnel connector token: decoded JSON {"a":"...","t":"...","s":"..."}
  - regex: \{"a":".+","t":".+","s":"[A-Za-z0-9+/=]{20,}"
    message: "Cloudflare tunnel connector token hidden in a base64 blob (decoded to {\"a\":...,\"s\":<secret>}). Rotate the credential and move it to a secret store. @see SECRETS-090, ADR-049"
  # Generic credential-shaped key/value inside decoded JSON or config.
  - regex: (?i)"(password|passwd|secret|token|api[_-]?key|private[_-]?key|access[_-]?key)"\s*:\s*"[^"]{12,}"
    message: "Credential-shaped value hidden one base64 layer deep. Decode locally to confirm, then rotate and move it to a secret store. @see SECRETS-090, ADR-049"
  # AWS access key id surfacing after decode.
  - regex: AKIA[0-9A-Z]{16}
    message: "AWS access key id revealed after base64 decode. Rotate immediately and move credentials to a secret store. @see SECRETS-090, ADR-049"
fix: "Decode the blob locally (never paste into an online decoder), confirm the secret, rotate it at the provider, and store it in a secret manager (AWS Secrets Manager / SSM / Vault) referenced at runtime — never hardcoded, even encoded."
